{%- comment -%}
An interactive illustration. Object + verb: an abstract shape held on the
monospace grid, and a thing the cursor does to it.

  {% include illustration name="brain-fry"
     alt="A dense field of marks. Sweeping removes the light ones; what
          survives is heavier."
     caption="Automation takes the easy decisions first." %}

  name     the illustration's profile in _data/illustrations.yml. Carries its
           object, verb, seed, svg and style. One line is the whole call.
  alt      required for a figure. Describes what the illustration shows and
           does. Content, so it stays at the call site, not in the profile.
  caption  optional. Visible, and carries the argument in words.

  Any profile field may be overridden inline for a one-off: object, verb, seed,
  fit, height, width, svg, background.

  STYLE lives in the profile's `style:` map, never here and never in a post.
  Each key becomes a data-illo-* attribute the engine reads, and an absent key
  means "ask the stylesheet". See _data/illustrations.yml, and the precedence
  rule in assets/js/illustration/config.js:

      attribute  >  scoped CSS  >  engine default

  Objects are listed in _data/illo_shapes.yml, verbs in
  assets/js/illustration/verbs/.

The static SVG below is what a reader without JavaScript, or with
prefers-reduced-motion, sees: the object as a dotted plate. The engine upgrades
it to a live canvas. Both read their geometry from the same path in
_data/illo_shapes.yml, so the two can never drift apart.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
Background mode defaults to `plate`, the one full-bleed object: a background's
edge is the edge of the viewport, so an inset shape reads as a rectangle
floating on the page rather than as ground. Every other object is inset by
design. Naming an object explicitly still works, and then it is not
edge-to-edge — which is the point of naming it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign _p = site.data.illustrations[include.name] -%}
{%- assign _background = include.background | default: _p.background -%}
{%- assign _object = include.object | default: _p.object -%}
{%- if _background and _object == nil -%}{%- assign _object = "plate" -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- assign _verb = include.verb | default: _p.verb | default: "order" -%}
{%- assign _seed = include.seed | default: _p.seed | default: 1 -%}
{%- assign _height = include.height | default: _p.height -%}
{%- assign _width = include.width | default: _p.width -%}
{%- assign _svg = include.svg | default: _p.svg -%}
{%- assign _shape = site.data.illo_shapes[_object] -%}
{%- assign _path = _shape.d -%}
{%- assign _fit = include.fit | default: _p.fit | default: _shape.fit | default: "contain" -%}
{%- assign _id = _object | append: "-" | append: _verb | append: "-" | append: _seed -%}
{%- comment -%}
The illustration's style, as attributes. Each key of the profile's `style:` map
becomes data-illo-<kebab>, which the engine reads without knowing the name: a
new parameter is a key here and a read in the rule, and nothing else changes.

A key absent from the profile emits no attribute, which is how an illustration
says "ask the stylesheet" — see the precedence rule in
assets/js/illustration/config.js.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture _style -%}
{%- for pair in _p.style %} data-illo-{{ pair[0] | replace: "_", "-" }}="{{ pair[1] }}"{% endfor -%}
{%- endcapture -%}
{%- if _path -%}
{%- if _background -%}
{%- comment -%}
Background mode. The same engine, the same object+verb, placed as a layer
rather than as a figure.

Three things are deliberately absent, and all three follow from the same fact:
a background makes its argument to a reader who is doing something else.

  no caption     there is nowhere to put one, and a background that needed a
                 caption to be understood would be the wrong background.
  no alt         it is aria-hidden. A figure's alt exists because the figure
                 carries content; this carries atmosphere for the argument the
                 page is already making in words.
  no static SVG  doctrine rule 14 requires an exported fallback for every
                 figure, because a reader without JavaScript would otherwise
                 lose part of the post. Here they lose nothing: no-JS and
                 reduced-motion readers get plain paper, which is what the page
                 looked like before this existed. A viewport-sized export would
                 also be ~40 KB of inline SVG on the most-visited page.

The pitch is not stated in the profile: a layer spans a viewport rather than a
column, and its grid is derived from the hero's own type metrics in .illo--bg
so the lattice is made of the type it sits behind.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="illo illo--bg"
     aria-hidden="true"
     data-illo="field"
     data-illo-render="{{ _verb }}"
     data-illo-path="{{ _path }}"
     data-illo-fit="{{ _fit }}"
     data-illo-seed="{{ _seed }}"{{ _style }}
     {% if _height %}style="--illo-h: {{ _height }}"{% endif %}>
  <div class="illo__stage"></div>
</div>
{%- else -%}
<figure class="illo{% if _width == 'wide' %} illo--wide{% endif %}"
        data-illo="field"
        data-illo-render="{{ _verb }}"
        data-illo-path="{{ _path }}"
        data-illo-fit="{{ _fit }}"
        data-illo-seed="{{ _seed }}"{{ _style }}
        {% if _height %}style="--illo-h: {{ _height }}"{% endif %}>
  <div class="illo__stage">
    {%- comment -%}
    The static fallback: what a reader without JavaScript, or with reduced
    motion, sees. Two grades of it.

    svg=<name>  An exported idle state from _includes/illustrations/<name>.svg,
                generated with Illo.exportSVG() against a settled illustration.
                This is the real thing: the actual marks the engine drew, so it
                shows this illustration rather than a generic plate. Prefer it.

    (none)      A dotted plate clipped to the object's outline. Correct but
                generic — two illustrations sharing an object look identical.
                Fine while authoring, before the export exists.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="illo__static" role="img"
         aria-label="{{ include.alt | strip_newlines | normalize_whitespace | escape }}">
      {%- if _svg -%}
      {% include illustrations/{{ _svg }}.svg %}
      {%- else -%}
      <svg viewBox="{% unless _fit == 'wide' %}0 0 100 100{% endunless %}"
           preserveAspectRatio="{% if _fit == 'wide' %}none{% else %}xMidYMid meet{% endif %}"
           aria-hidden="true">
        <defs>
          {%- if _fit == 'wide' %}
          <pattern id="illo-grid-{{ _id }}" width="8" height="8" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
            <rect width="3.4" height="3.4" x="2.3" y="2.3" fill="currentColor"/>
          </pattern>
          <clipPath id="illo-clip-{{ _id }}" clipPathUnits="objectBoundingBox">
            <path d="{{ _path }}" transform="scale(0.01)"/>
          </clipPath>
          {%- else %}
          <pattern id="illo-grid-{{ _id }}" width="2.9" height="2.9" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
            <rect width="1.25" height="1.25" x="0.83" y="0.83" fill="currentColor"/>
          </pattern>
          {%- endif %}
        </defs>
        {%- if _fit == 'wide' %}
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#illo-grid-{{ _id }})"
              clip-path="url(#illo-clip-{{ _id }})"/>
        {%- else %}
        <path d="{{ _path }}" fill="url(#illo-grid-{{ _id }})"/>
        {%- endif %}
      </svg>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
  {%- if include.caption %}
  <figcaption>{{ include.caption | markdownify | remove: "<p>" | remove: "</p>" | strip }}</figcaption>
  {%- endif %}
</figure>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
